// @test: typecheck-fail
// @expected-error: "cannot bind in a plain `let`"
// @level: L1
// @tier: 0
// @tags: patterns, let, refutable
// @timeout: 20000
// @description: A literal, range or or-pattern in a plain `let` is refused —
//   a `let` matches ONE value and the pattern must always match.

// Measured 2026-09-12, before the check existed:
//
//     let n = 5;
//     let 7 = n;        verum check   clean
//     print("bound")    interpreted   prints "bound"
//
// Nothing matched and nothing said so. E429 — "a refutable pattern in a
// position that must always match" — already existed and already had the
// predicate; it had exactly ONE emit site, function parameters, pinned by
// `refutable_parameter_pattern_is_refused.vr` beside this file. The `let`
// position is the sibling case the same reasoning covers.
//
// Radius measured before landing: `core/` carries zero such patterns, and
// every occurrence in the spec corpus is in a parse-fail or parse-only
// spec that never reaches the type checker.
//
// NOT COVERED HERE, and deliberately: a VARIANT pattern.
// `let Maybe.Some(x) = v;` is still accepted and still binds an unchecked
// payload. Refutability for a variant needs the RESOLVED type — a
// single-variant newtype destructured by name is irrefutable and must stay
// legal — and the predicate behind this check reads syntax alone. That
// half is open.

fn main() {
    let n = 5;

    // A literal matches one value out of many.
    let 7 = n;

    // A range is the same objection over more values.
    let 1..=9 = n;

    // An or-pattern offers alternatives, so an alternative can fail.
    let 1 | 2 = n;

    print(n);
}
